        <section id="build" aria-labelledby="build-title">
          <h1 class="page-title" id="build-title">Building a site</h1>
          <p class="intro">Read this when you turn your own image into a site. The Dockerfile describes the app.
            Labels in it, or flags on <code>snowglobe build</code>, tell snowglobe how to snapshot it.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="dockerfile">Describe it in the Dockerfile</h2>
          <p>Labels let an image describe itself. A flag on the command line overrides its label. This is the
            Python demo's whole Dockerfile:</p>
          <pre>FROM alpine:3.24.2
RUN apk add --no-cache python3
CMD ["python3"]
LABEL snowglobe.title="Python in the browser" \
      snowglobe.ready=">>> " \
      snowglobe.exercise='["import json, re, collections", "import asyncio; asyncio.run(asyncio.sleep(0))"]'</pre>
          <p>Every flag and its label are in the <a href="../reference/#build-flags">build flags</a> table.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="ready">When the snapshot is taken</h2>
          <p>snowglobe boots the image until the app is ready, then saves the whole machine. Visitors restore that
            snapshot instead of booting Linux.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>With <code>--ready TEXT</code></strong> (or <code>snowglobe.ready</code>), the app is ready
              once it prints that text. Use its prompt.</li>
            <li><strong>Without it</strong>, the app counts as ready once its terminal has been quiet for 5
              seconds.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><code>--cmd</code> swaps the program without touching the image. It also drops the image's
            <code>ready</code> and <code>exercise</code> labels, which belonged to the image's own command.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="freed-memory">Freed memory stays out of the snapshot</h2>
          <p>Memory the guest freed would still hold what it held: a package manager's caches, a compiler's
            work, a secret a setup step read. The guest's kernel zeroes each page as it frees it
            (<code>init_on_free</code>), so none of that reaches the snapshot. A site with a kernel also has free page
            reporting: the kernel reports the free memory to the machine in 2 MiB blocks, and the machine gives
            those pages back. The build lets the guest idle a few seconds before saving, so the last of it is
            reported. The guest keeps all of its memory: it can use those pages again at any time.</p>
          <p>A snapshot restores only onto the devices it was saved with, so sites built before this have no such
            device and restore as they are. Rebuild a site to make its snapshot smaller.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="exercises">Exercises and the warm pack</h2>
          <p>The guest fetches its files on demand. To hide that wait, every site preloads a
            <code>warm.pack</code> in the background. snowglobe fills it by measuring, not guessing, with what a
            restored machine reads, in the order it first reads it:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Restore reads:</strong> the build drops the guest's file cache before the snapshot, as a
              visitor's machine starts. After saving it, snowglobe lets the machine run on its own for a few
              seconds and records what its services read.</li>
            <li><strong>Exercise reads:</strong> then it types each <code>--exercise</code>
              command into the app and records what it reads. These are the files a visitor's first commands
              would otherwise wait for. A command is done once the app prints its prompt again and goes
              quiet.</li>
            <li><strong>Pattern:</strong> anything matching <code>--warm REGEX</code>, for files no exercise
              touches, after the rest.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>What only booting read stays out of the pack: the restored machine has it in memory already, or
            never reads it again. A site with no exercises (or built with <code>--sandbox</code>) packs its boot
            reads after the rest, the best guess there is.</p>
          <p>The page reads the pack as it streams in, so the files the guest needs first arrive first. A read of a
            file the pack brings soon waits for it; one far off (a visitor whose first command isn't the build's) is
            fetched on its own, and the pack holds back while the guest waits on such fetches, so they have the
            bandwidth. Files the machine's own cache lets go come again from the pack, or from the copy the page
            keeps of files it fetched on their own, never from the network a second time.</p>
          <p>Each build prints a breakdown and writes <code>warm-report.json</code>: what the restored machine read
            settling, what each exercise read first, what only booting read, the pattern's files and the files
            never read.</p>
          <p>An app used over the network, not through its console, can be warmed from real traffic instead.
            Record its reads while something talks to it, then add them to the pack:</p>
          <pre>snowglobe run dist --name api --detach -p 5000:5000 --record-reads api-reads.json
npm test                                    <span class="dim"># anything that talks to 127.0.0.1:5000</span>
snowglobe stop api &amp;&amp; snowglobe warm dist api-reads.json</pre>

          <h2 class="h3" id="cpus">CPUs and memory</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><code>--memory MB</code> sets the guest's RAM (512 by default).</li>
            <li><code>--cpus N</code> (1 to 8) gives it more CPUs. Each runs in its own Web Worker on shared memory,
              which helps programs that use them.</li>
            <li>A build boots on at least 4 CPUs (<code>--build-cpus</code>) and takes the extra ones offline before
              the snapshot.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Browsers only share memory between workers on a cross-origin isolated page, and a static host can't
            declare one. So a site on several CPUs ships a small service worker that reloads the page once on the
            first visit. Such a site can't run inside another page's frame.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="image-services">Services in the image</h2>
          <p>The guest boots with BusyBox's <code>init</code> and no service manager, so the app is ready seconds
            after the kernel starts.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Services an image enables for OpenRC do not start. Start them from the command, as the Postgres and
              Elixir examples' start scripts do.</li>
            <li>Slow first-time setup (an <code>initdb</code>, migrations) belongs in a Dockerfile <code>RUN</code>.
              There it runs at native speed instead of in emulation.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="h3" id="without-docker">Building without Docker</h2>
          <p><code>--builder native</code> (or <code>SNOWGLOBE_BUILDER=native</code>) builds from either:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>an image reference, pulled from its registry, or</li>
            <li>an image archive: <code>docker save</code>, or an OCI layout from buildah, kaniko or anything
              else.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Nothing runs at build time either way. The image's layers are flattened, the prebuilt kernel and boot
            files go on top, and <code>snowglobe-vm</code> boots and snapshots it on your machine.</p>
          <pre>snowglobe build python:3.13-alpine --builder native --ready '>>> '
docker save my-app:latest -o my-app.tar         <span class="dim"># on a machine with a builder</span>
snowglobe build my-app.tar --builder native     <span class="dim"># anywhere else</span></pre>

          <h2 class="h3" id="hosting">Hosting several sites, and installing them</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><code>snowglobe pool &lt;dir&gt;</code> lets every site under a directory share one blob store. Blobs
              are named by content, so sites built on the same base share many, and a visitor's second site reuses
              what their browser cached.</li>
            <li><code>snowglobe pwa &lt;site&gt;</code> makes a site an installable app that keeps working offline
              once it has run. It adds a manifest, icons and a service worker that caches the page, the machine and
              every file the run fetched. The page asks for its build's own files (the snapshot, the filesystem
              tree, the warm pack, the machine and its scripts) with that build's version (<code>?v=</code>), so a
              repeat visit gets them from that cache instead of downloading them again, and a newer build's page asks
              for other URLs and never gets an older build's copy. Run it after <code>pool</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Next: give the guest a network on <a href="../networking/">Networking</a>, or run the site without a
            browser on <a href="../running/">Running outside a browser</a>.</p>
        </section>
